      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "\\[\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ} &= \\frac{\\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ}{\\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 80^\\circ - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos 80^\\circ \\right)}{\\sin 160^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\left( \\sin 10^\\circ \\cos 80^\\circ - \\cos 10^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\right)}{\\sin 200^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\sin \\left ( 10^\\circ - 80^\\circ \\right )}{\\sin 200^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2 \\sin 70^\\circ}{\\sin 50^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2 \\cos 20^\\circ}{\\sin 50^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2 \\cos 20^\\circ}{\\cos 40^\\circ} \\\\\n&= -2 \\boxed{\\frac{\\sin 40^\\circ}{\\cos 40^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ}}.\n\\end{aligned}\\]\n(China, Mathematics Kangaroo Contest, Grade 10, Question 26, 1973)\n\nTo solve the given expression \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ}\\), we will proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the expression:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nTo combine these terms, we need a common denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ}{\\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify the numerator \\(\\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ\\). We recognize that this can be expressed using a trigonometric identity. Specifically, we use the angle subtraction formula for sine:\n\\[\n\\sin (A - B) = \\sin A \\cos B - \\cos A \\sin B\n\\]\n\nWe set \\(A = 60^\\circ\\) and \\(B = 20^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n\\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ = \\sin (80^\\circ - 20^\\circ) = \\sin 60^\\circ\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\). Therefore, the numerator simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute this back into our expression:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}{\\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nWe simplify the denominator using the double-angle identity for sine:\n\\[\n\\sin 2x = 2 \\sin x \\cos x\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin 80^\\circ \\cos 80^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 160^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sin 160^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 20^\\circ) = \\sin 20^\\circ\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\sin 80^\\circ \\cos 80^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 20^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus, the denominator becomes:\n\\[\n\\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 20^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this back into the expression, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}{\\frac{1}{2} \\sin 20^\\circ} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sin 20^\\circ} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 20^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\sin 20^\\circ = 2 \\sin 10^\\circ \\cos 10^\\circ\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 20^\\circ} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2 \\sin 10^\\circ \\cos 10^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nUsing the double-angle identity for cosine:\n\\[\n\\cos 2x = 1 - 2 \\sin^2 x\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos 20^\\circ = 1 - 2 \\sin^2 10^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2 \\sin 10^\\circ \\cos 10^\\circ} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 20^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 20^\\circ} = \\frac{\\cos 40^\\circ}{\\sin 40^\\circ} = \\cot 40^\\circ\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
